    "photo": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Marcus_Aurelius_Metropolitan_Museum.png",
    "body": "Marcus Aurelius (121-180 CE) was a Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher whose private notebook, later titled the *Meditations*, gives a rare view of how someone might actually try to live a Stoic life. The last of Rome's so-called Five Good Emperors, he has stood in the Western imagination for the golden age of the empire, and his book has been read for generations as the reflections of a philosopher on the throne.\n\n## Biographical Details\n\nHe was born in 121 CE into a prominent Roman family, related through his grandparents to several of the leading houses of the day. His early schooling was overseen by the emperor Hadrian, and the succession soon turned in his favor: when Hadrian's chosen heir died in 138, the emperor adopted Antoninus Pius on the condition that Antoninus in turn adopt the teenage Marcus. Marked out for the throne at just under seventeen, he served a long apprenticeship at Antoninus's side and received the formal powers of emperor in 147. In 145 he married Antoninus's daughter, his cousin Annia Galeria Faustina, with whom he had at least twelve children, among them his successor Commodus.\n\nTrained in rhetoric by his teacher Fronto, with whom he kept up a lifelong correspondence, Marcus grew restless with declamation and turned instead to philosophy, seizing on the *Discourses* of the former slave Epictetus. He became emperor in 161 and, at his own insistence, shared power with his adoptive brother Lucius Verus. His reign gave him little peace: war with Parthia, a plague carried home by the returning armies, and repeated German invasions across the Danube kept him on campaign in central Europe for much of it. He still found time to endow four chairs of philosophy in Athens, one each for the Platonic, Aristotelian, Stoic, and Epicurean traditions. He died in 180.\n\n## Key Ideas\n\nMarcus is not a system-builder, and the *Meditations* argues for no doctrine. It is better understood as a set of exercises - writing used to drill Stoic ideas into himself until they reshaped his character and conduct. He took his cue from Epictetus, who held that the student of philosophy must train in three areas: desire, the impulse to act, and assent to impressions, matching the older Stoic division into physics, ethics, and logic. Knowing the theory was never enough; it had to be worked into habit.\n\nAt the heart of the ethics Marcus inherited is the claim that only virtue is good and only vice bad, while everything people ordinarily chase or dread - wealth, reputation, health, and their opposites - is indifferent to happiness. Since fortune scatters these things among good and bad alike, he argues, they cannot be genuine goods. Happiness depends not on what befalls a person but on the judgments they make about it. Following Epictetus, he holds that we are disturbed not by things but by our opinions of them, and so he tells himself again and again to \"erase\" his impressions - to strip away the unexamined value judgments folded into how things first appear.\n\nTwo further commitments give the work its shape. One is justice: Marcus treats the cosmos as a city and every rational being as a fellow-citizen, so that one's task is to act for the good of the whole and to hate no one. The other is piety: because the universe is run by an intelligent providence, whatever happens to a part serves the whole, and the right response to one's lot is not grudging acceptance but a kind of welcome. When he wavers, he sets himself a stark choice - \"providence or atoms?\" - noting that whether the world is ordered by reason or thrown together by colliding atoms, complaint makes no sense either way.\n\n## Major Works\n\nMarcus's philosophical reputation rests on a single book. The *Meditations* - its Greek title, *ta eis heauton*, means simply \"to himself\" - was written for no reader but its author, probably during his campaigns around 171-175. The entries follow no clear plan and may stand in the order he composed them, circling back to death, the faults of others, and the smallness of human affairs; Book One stands apart as a record of debts to the relatives and teachers who shaped him. Beyond it, part of his correspondence with Fronto survives, recovered from a reused manuscript in 1815, though it tells us more about the man than about his philosophy.\n\n## Quotes\n\nWaste no more time arguing what a good man should be. Be one.\n\n*— Meditations*\n\nAll is as thinking makes it so.\n\n*— Meditations, 2.15*\n\nThe defining characteristic of the good person is to love and embrace whatever happens to him along his thread of fate.\n\n*— Meditations, 3.16.2*",
