    "photo": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Sextus.jpg",
    "body": "Sextus Empiricus (fl. late second or early third century CE) was a Greek physician and Pyrrhonist philosopher whose surviving books are the fullest account of ancient skepticism to come down to us. Because he lays out the doctrines of rival schools in order to argue against them, those books are also one of our best sources for Hellenistic philosophy as a whole. The skepticism he defends takes its name from [[Pyrrho]].\n\n## Biographical Details\n\nAlmost nothing is known about his life, which is fitting given his philosophy. Scholars cannot fix where or when he lived; the best guesses place him anywhere between 100 CE and the first half of the third century, though he seems to have been well known already by the century's end. He probably worked in Alexandria, Rome, or Athens. His Roman name suggests he held Roman citizenship. The surname \"Empiricus\" marks his profession: he belonged to the Empirical school of medicine, one of the three medical schools of the day, alongside the Rationalists and the Methodists. This leaves a standing puzzle, since in the Outlines he remarks that Pyrrhonism actually sits closer to the Methodist school than to his own. A late Byzantine encyclopedia, the Suda, identified him with a certain Sextus of Chaeronea, but scholars generally doubt the identification.\n\n## Key Ideas\n\nFor Sextus, skepticism is not a body of doctrine but a skill. The word itself comes from the Greek for inquiry, and the skeptic is simply someone who keeps investigating. Where dogmatic philosophers believe they have found the truth and Academic skeptics insist it cannot be found, the Pyrrhonist goes on looking. What marks him out is an ability: for any argument that something is the case, he can produce an equally convincing argument that it is not. When the two sides balance exactly — Sextus calls this equipollence — the mind can no longer come down on either, and judgment is suspended (epoché).\n\nHe set this apart sharply from the Academic skepticism of Carneades, which held that nothing can be known. To assert even that, Sextus objected, is to make a firm claim of the very kind the skeptic avoids. The Pyrrhonist affirms nothing, not even his own doubt; he simply withholds assent.\n\nThe point of all this is tranquillity (ataraxia). People take up the skeptical life hoping that settling life's contradictions will free them from disturbance; instead they find the questions cannot be settled, suspend judgment, and discover to their surprise that calm follows anyway. Sextus says it arrives \"as a shadow follows a body,\" and illustrates the point with the painter Apelles, who despaired of rendering the foam on a horse's mouth and flung his sponge at the picture, only for the smear to produce exactly the effect he had failed to paint. Peace comes precisely when one stops chasing it.\n\nTo bring suspension of judgment about, the skeptic uses sets of arguments called Modes. The Ten Modes, inherited from Aenesidemus, work on the relativity of appearances: honey tastes sweet to the healthy and bitter to the jaundiced, a tower looks round from a distance and square up close, so no appearance can be trusted to reveal how a thing really is. The more searching Five Modes, ascribed to Agrippa, press any claim toward one of three dead ends — an endless regress of justifications, a circular argument that assumes what it sets out to prove, or a bare assumption asserted without proof.\n\nNone of this stops the skeptic from living an ordinary life. In a much-debated passage Sextus explains that skeptics do hold beliefs of a kind: they assent to the feelings that appearances force upon them, so that a person who feels cold will not deny feeling cold, while withholding assent from the \"unclear\" matters that theory and science argue over. Guided by how things appear, by bodily need, by custom, and by training, the skeptic eats, worships, and works like anyone else, but keeps no opinions about the hidden nature of things.\n\n## Major Works\n\nTwo bodies of work survive. The Outlines of Pyrrhonism, in three books, is the fuller and more accessible: the first book describes what Pyrrhonism is, while the second and third turn its methods loose on the logic, physics, and ethics of the dogmatists. The second work is known by its Latin title Adversus Mathematicos (\"Against the Mathematicians,\" or more loosely \"Against the Professors\"), and runs to eleven books in the manuscripts. Six of them attack the liberal disciplines one by one — grammar, rhetoric, geometry, arithmetic, astrology, and music — while the remaining five, sometimes grouped separately as Against the Dogmatists, take on the logicians, physicists, and ethicists. The surviving text is incomplete and refers to other writings of his now lost, among them medical notes and notes on the soul.\n\n## Quotes\n\nWhen we say that Skeptics do not hold beliefs, we do not take 'belief' in the sense in which some say, quite generally, that belief is acquiescing in something; for Skeptics assent to the feelings forced upon them by appearances—for example, they would not say, when heated or chilled, 'I think I am not heated (or: chilled)'. Rather, we say that they do not hold beliefs in the sense in which some say that belief is assent to some unclear object of investigation in the sciences; for Pyrrhonists do not assent to anything unclear.\n\n*— Outlines of Pyrrhonism, I.13*\n\nWe do not study natural science in order to make assertions with firm conviction about any of the matters on which scientific beliefs are held. But we do touch on natural science in order to be able to oppose to every account an equal account, and for the sake of tranquillity. This is also the spirit in which we approach the logical and ethical parts of what they call philosophy.\n\n*— Outlines of Pyrrhonism, I.18*",
