    "body": "Plotinus (204–270 CE) is regarded as the founder of Neoplatonism, the school of thought that reworked [[Plato]]'s philosophy into a systematic account of how everything that exists flows from a single, transcendent source. Teaching in Rome, he built a philosophy that would shape medieval and Renaissance thought, though his own concern was narrower: the soul's return to what he called the One.\n\n## Biographical Details\n\nPlotinus was born in 204 CE in Egypt; the exact location is not recorded. At the age of twenty-eight he went to Alexandria and sampled several teachers before finding one who satisfied him, Ammonius Saccas, with whom he studied for roughly a decade. In the early 240s he left Ammonius to join an expedition the emperor Gordian III led against Persia, aiming, according to Porphyry, to meet the philosophers of Persia and India in person. The campaign never reached its goal: Gordian was killed by his own soldiers in Mesopotamia, and Plotinus made his way to Rome instead.\n\nHe settled there in his early forties, opened a school, and taught for more than two decades, drawing students to lectures built around close discussion of Plato, Aristotle, and their commentators. About ten years after settling in Rome, he began writing down his teaching as formal treatises. Porphyry, who studied with him for some five years, encouraged him and later became his biographer, editor, and the organizer of his collected works. By his final years his handwriting had grown barely legible, a consequence of failing sight. He died in 270 CE, reportedly in the region of Campania.\n\n## Key Ideas\n\nAt the center of Plotinus's system are three fundamental realities, arranged in a strict hierarchy: the One, the Intelligence, and the Soul. The One is beyond being itself — not a thing among things but the source that makes all things possible, so far beyond ordinary description that Plotinus says even calling it \"the One\" falls short of what it is. Because it is perfect and complete, it overflows without any loss to itself, and this overflow gives rise to the Intelligence, which holds the Platonic Forms as its own thoughts. The Intelligence in turn gives rise to the Soul, which stands between the eternal, unchanging realm above it and the changing physical world below.\n\nPlotinus held that everything short of the One, matter included, was in some sense good, since everything owes its existence, however remotely, to that source. This put him at odds with the Gnostics of his day, who held that the visible world was the botched work of an inferior, malevolent power, in which the soul found itself stranded. Plotinus rejected this and defended the order and beauty of the physical cosmos. Matter, in his account, was not an active evil force but a kind of privation — an absence of form so complete that it has no positive qualities of its own. Evil arises only when the soul, distracted by matter, forgets what it truly is and loses sight of the Good.\n\nHe also broke from earlier accounts of sense-perception, which treated the mind as a passive recipient of impressions stamped onto it from outside. Perception, he argued, is active: the soul turns the body's raw disturbances into intelligible images it can judge and use. Genuine knowledge belongs to the higher part of the soul, which never fully leaves its contemplation of the Intelligence; the lower part that engages directly with the body can produce only opinion.\n\nFor Plotinus the goal of a human life is \"likeness to God as far as possible,\" reached not through action in the world but through the soul's inward turn back toward its source. He distinguished lesser virtues, which discipline behavior in society, from higher virtues, which free the soul from concern with the body and fix it instead on contemplation of the intelligible. Beauty plays a central role in this ascent: earthly beauty, whether in a face or in music, is only a reflection of that changeless, intelligible Beauty, and the soul's love of it is really an unrecognized longing for its true home. The soul that attains this vision finds a happiness that nothing befalling the body can disturb.\n\n## Major Works\n\nPlotinus's entire surviving output is gathered in the Enneads, fifty-four treatises edited after his death by Porphyry. The name reflects Porphyry's decision to sort the material into six sets of nine treatises each: he put the more approachable material on ethics and human life first, saving the hardest treatises — on the Soul, the Intelligence, and the One — for last. Porphyry also kept a separate record of the order in which the treatises were actually composed, which some modern scholars prefer to his thematic arrangement. Their length is wildly uneven, some barely a page and others running past a hundred, and they read less like a finished system than transcripts of live argument, circling back to the same questions from new angles. Porphyry's own preface to the collection, the Life of Plotinus, remains the principal source for nearly everything known about its author.\n\n## Quotes\n\nBeauty for the most part, consists in objects of sight; but it is also received through the ears, by the skilful composition of words, and the consonant proportion of sounds; for in every species of harmony, beauty is to be found. And if we rise from sense into the regions of soul, we shall there perceive studies and offices, actions and habits, sciences and virtues, invested with a much larger portion of beauty. But whether there is above these, a still higher beauty, will appear as we advance in its investigation.\n\n*— An Essay on the Beautiful*\n\nRecall your thoughts inward, and if while contemplating yourself, you do not perceive yourself beautiful, imitate the statuary; who when he desires a beautiful statue cuts away what is superfluous, smooths and polishes what is rough, and never desists until he has given it all the beauty his art is able to effect.\n\n*— An Essay on the Beautiful*",
