    "photo": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Saint_Augustine_by_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg",
    "body": "Augustine of Hippo (354–430) was a bishop, theologian, and philosopher in Roman North Africa whose writings shaped Western Christianity for over a thousand years and left a lasting mark on philosophy. Trained in classical rhetoric and steeped in the works of [[Cicero]] and the Neoplatonist [[Plotinus]], he turned that inheritance toward questions of knowledge, will, evil, and time that philosophers argued over a millennium later.\n\n## Biographical Details\n\nAugustine was born in 354 in Thagaste, a modest town in Roman Numidia, now Algeria. His mother, Monnica, was a devout Christian; his father, Patricius, was baptized only on his deathbed. Strained family resources funded an education in rhetoric at Madauros and Carthage, meant to launch him into imperial administration. In Carthage, around eighteen, he took a lover whose name has not survived; they lived together some fourteen years and had a son, Adeodatus. He also joined Manicheism, a dualist sect explaining evil as the work of a rival dark power, staying with it about nine years over his mother's objections.\n\nAt twenty-eight he left for Rome, then took a post as imperial professor of rhetoric in Milan, where the bishop Ambrose taught him an allegorical way of reading Scripture and Christian Neoplatonists gave him a satisfying alternative to Manicheism. After a long spell of indecision he later narrated as a kind of illness, he converted in 386 and was baptized by Ambrose the next Easter. Returning to Africa, he lost his mother on the journey, at Ostia; Adeodatus died not long after, in his late teens. Augustine was then pressed, against his wishes, into the priesthood at Hippo Regius in 391 and made bishop there within a few years, an office he held for life, absorbed in controversy against the Manicheans he had left, the Donatists, and finally Pelagius, who held that people could choose the good without divine grace. The Vandals were besieging Hippo when he died in 430; tradition holds he spent his last days reciting the Penitential Psalms.\n\n## Key Ideas\n\n**Certainty against skepticism.** Cicero's dialogue Hortensius first drew Augustine to philosophy, and he spent years wrestling with the Academic skeptics, who held certain knowledge unreachable. His answer: locate a zone of certainty within the self. Even someone deceived about everything else cannot be deceived about her own existence, since being deceived at all requires existing. The argument anticipates Descartes' cogito by twelve centuries, though Augustine built on it a theory of divine illumination, in which the mind grasps unchanging truths of mathematics, logic, and morality not from the senses but by consulting an inner light identified with God.\n\n**Will, evil, and grace.** Against the Manichean claim that evil is a substance contending with good, Augustine argued that evil has no positive existence: it is a falling-away, a corruption of a nature that remains good insofar as it exists. Applied to wrongdoing, this located sin in a free will turning from a greater good toward a lesser one for no cause beyond its own choice. Yet his reading of Paul convinced him that since Adam's disobedience the will cannot turn itself back to God unaided; freedom to do good must be restored by grace given without regard to merit. This conviction, hardened in the dispute with Pelagius, committed Augustine to a doctrine of predestination that would later split the Reformation churches.\n\n**The two cities.** In The City of God, begun after the Gothic sack of Rome in 410, Augustine treats human history as the story of two intermingled populations distinguished only by what they love: those whose love is fixed on God belong to the city of God, and those whose love is fixed on themselves belong to the earthly city. Piety in its rulers does not turn any earthly empire into the city of God. Political order is not sacred in itself; at best it restrains the worst effects of a fallen humanity, worth pursuing but never mistaken for salvation.\n\n**Time.** In Book 11 of the Confessions, Augustine asks what time is and concludes that past and future exist only as they are present to the mind, in memory and expectation, while the present itself passes too quickly to have duration. Time, on this account, is less a feature of the outer world than a stretching or \"distention\" of the soul between what it remembers and what it anticipates.\n\n## Major Works\n\nAugustine's output was immense — over a hundred books, hundreds of letters and sermons — but two works stand out. The Confessions, composed around 396 to 400, recount his early life and conversion as an extended address to God, weaving autobiography with reflection on memory, time, and grace. The City of God, his longest work, occupied him from 412 to 426; it defends Christianity against the charge that it weakened Rome and casts history as the story of two cities pursuing incompatible loves. Also notable are On the Trinity, on the mind as an image of its creator; On Christian Doctrine, a handbook of biblical interpretation; and On Free Choice of the Will, on the origin of evil. Near the end of his life he compiled the Retractations, revisiting and correcting his own earlier positions.",
